 stem:'A 68-year-old with obesity and diabetes returns after a third episode of vaginal spotting in 4 months. Examination shows blood from the cervical os without a visible cervical lesion. Transvaginal ultrasonography previously showed a 3-mm endometrial stripe, and an adequate office biopsy showed benign tissue. She takes no anticoagulant or hormone therapy. Hemoglobin and vital signs are normal. Which next step best addresses the continuing symptoms?',
 choices:[['Arrange hysteroscopy with directed endometrial assessment and sampling','Recurrent bleeding warrants further evaluation because a thin stripe and benign blind sample can miss focal or otherwise inadequately sampled pathology.'],['Reassure her permanently because a stripe under 4 mm excludes every cancer','High negative predictive value is not perfect exclusion, and persistent symptoms change the clinical question despite an initially reassuring measurement.'],['Start systemic estrogen alone without additional evaluation','Treating presumed atrophy without resolving recurrent uterine bleeding can delay diagnosis, and unopposed systemic estrogen can stimulate the endometrium.'],['Repeat cervical cytology as the sole evaluation','Cervical cytology does not adequately evaluate the endometrial cavity and cannot substitute for investigating recurrent bleeding arising from the uterus.']],
 explanation:'Persistent or recurrent postmenopausal bleeding merits additional endometrial evaluation even after initially reassuring tests. Blind sampling can miss a focal lesion, and a thin stripe does not exclude every endometrial malignancy. Hysteroscopy permits cavity visualization and directed sampling. This case already includes both imaging and tissue assessment, avoiding reliance on older ultrasound-only initial pathways. Stable hemoglobin supports organized outpatient evaluation but does not justify abandoning the workup. Decisions about treating atrophy should follow an adequate assessment of the recurrent bleeding.',
 objective:'Respond to recurrent postmenopausal bleeding despite a prior benign blind sample and thin stripe.',takeaways:['Persistent symptoms can outweigh reassuring initial screening characteristics.','Directed assessment addresses lesions that blind sampling may miss.'],references:[{label:'ACOG 2026 update: evaluation of postmenopausal bleeding',url:'https://www.acog.org/news/news-releases/2026/04/acog-publishes-updated-guidance-evaluation-postmenopausal-bleeding'},{label:'ACOG: role of transvaginal ultrasonography in postmenopausal bleeding',url:'https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2018/05/the-role-of-transvaginal-ultrasonography-in-evaluating-the-endometrium-of-women-with-postmenopausal-bleeding'}],evidenceNote:limitation,concerns:['2026 ACOG update was located; clinician must verify the current full-text pathway. The older 2018 guidance is supplementary, not claimed as the sole current authority.']
